The table itself is almost always an anonymous struct type, defined inline as a slice literal, with a field for whatever the case actually needs -- one or more inputs, an expected output, and, critically, a **name** for the case:

```go
func TestIsPalindrome(t *testing.T) {
	cases := []struct {
		name string
		input string
		want  bool
	}{
		{name: "empty string", input: "", want: true},
		{name: "single character", input: "a", want: true},
		{name: "simple palindrome", input: "racecar", want: true},
		{name: "non-palindrome", input: "hello", want: false},
		{name: "mixed case palindrome", input: "RaceCar", want: false},   // case-sensitive by design here
	}

	for _, tc := range cases {
		t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {
			got := IsPalindrome(tc.input)
			if got != tc.want {
				t.Errorf("IsPalindrome(%q) = %v, want %v", tc.input, got, tc.want)
			}
		})
	}
}
```

The `name` field is not optional decoration -- it's what turns an opaque numbered failure ("case 3 of 5 failed") into a self-describing one ("mixed case palindrome failed"), directly in `go test -v` output, with zero need to cross-reference back to the table's source. Some codebases use a `map[string]struct{...}` instead of a slice, with the map key doubling as the case name -- this has the side benefit of making duplicate case names a compile-time-detectable... actually a runtime-obvious issue (a duplicate map key silently overwrites the earlier entry) and randomizes case execution order on each run (map iteration order is intentionally randomized in Go), which can be a deliberate feature if you want to catch cases that accidentally depend on running in a particular order.

Table-driven tests very commonly call `t.Parallel()` as the first line inside each subtest's callback, to let independent cases run concurrently and speed up a large table:

```go
for _, tc := range cases {
	t.Run(tc.name, func(t *testing.T) {
		t.Parallel()   // this subtest may not actually run until later, after the loop has moved on
		got := IsPalindrome(tc.input)
		if got != tc.want {
			t.Errorf("IsPalindrome(%q) = %v, want %v", tc.input, got, tc.want)
		}
	})
}
```

Here's the subtlety worth understanding precisely: **without `t.Parallel()`, `t.Run` is synchronous** -- it blocks until the subtest's callback fully returns before the loop moves on to the next `tc` -- so even on older Go versions, a plain non-parallel table-driven test was never actually vulnerable to the classic loop-variable-capture bug from the Closures/Concurrency material, because the closure runs to completion (reading `tc` correctly) before `tc` is ever reassigned. The bug specifically appears once `t.Parallel()` is added: calling it signals "pause me here, let the surrounding loop continue immediately, and actually run my body later, once the parent test's other subtests reach their own end" -- which means, on Go versions before 1.22, every parallel subtest's closure could end up reading whatever value the single shared `tc` variable happened to hold by the time they all actually got around to running, typically the table's very last entry, for every single case.

**Go 1.22 (2024) fixed this at the language level**, the same way it fixed the analogous goroutine-closure bug: `for _, tc := range cases` now gives every iteration its own independent `tc`, so this code is correct as written on Go 1.22+. On an older Go version (or a `go.mod` pinning an older language version), the fix is the same explicit-parameter idiom from the closures material: `tc := tc` shadowed inside the loop body (a very common, slightly odd-looking but standard one-liner), or passing `tc` explicitly into the subtest closure as its own parameter. Because table-driven code is copied, adapted, and shared across codebases and tutorials spanning many Go versions, it remains good practice to write the shadowing line explicitly even on newer Go, purely so the code's correctness doesn't depend on a reader knowing which language version fixed this.

