A benchmark function has the shape `func BenchmarkXxx(b *testing.B)`, and its defining feature is a loop that you write yourself, iterating exactly `b.N` times:

```go
func BenchmarkFibonacci10(b *testing.B) {
	for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
		Fibonacci(10)
	}
}
```

The crucial detail, and the source of real confusion for newcomers, is that **you never decide what `b.N` is** -- the `testing` package's benchmark runner does, dynamically, at runtime. It starts by running your loop with a small `b.N` (often 1), times how long that took, and if the elapsed time was too short to measure reliably, it multiplies `b.N` up and runs your entire function again from scratch, repeating this process until it lands on an `N` large enough that the total elapsed time is long enough to produce a stable, low-noise measurement (roughly a second's worth, by default, though this is itself tunable). This is precisely why the loop body must be written as `for i := 0; i < b.N; i++`, rather than some hardcoded count you picked -- the whole calibration mechanism depends on being able to re-run your function with successively larger `N` values until the numbers stabilize; a hardcoded bound would just report the same tiny, statistically noisy duration on every calibration pass, defeating the entire point.

A consequence worth internalizing: **your `BenchmarkXxx` function's body may run multiple times in a single `go test -bench` invocation**, once per calibration attempt, and only the final, stabilized run's numbers are what actually get reported. Any code inside the benchmark function but outside the `for i := 0; i < b.N; i++` loop -- typically one-time setup -- runs freshly on every one of those calibration attempts too, which is exactly the problem `b.ResetTimer` exists to address, covered further down.

This is the single most infamous Go benchmarking pitfall, and it produces results that look great and are completely meaningless. If a benchmark calls a function purely for its side effect on the CPU but never does anything with the returned value, an optimizing compiler is, in principle, permitted to notice that the computed result is never observed anywhere and eliminate some or all of the actual work -- especially for small functions eligible for inlining, where the call boundary that would normally force the compiler to "not know" what happens to the result disappears entirely:

```go
// SUSPECT: the result of fib(30) is computed and then thrown away every iteration
func BenchmarkFibBad(b *testing.B) {
	for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
		fib(30)
	}
}
```

Depending on the compiler version and exactly how `fib` is written, this can report an implausibly, suspiciously fast time -- sometimes fast enough that a careful reader should immediately be suspicious rather than pleased. The standard defense is the **sink pattern**: assign the loop's result to a package-level variable at the very end, which forces the compiler to treat the computed value as genuinely observable (it could, for all the compiler can prove, be read by some other part of the program later), so it can no longer justify skipping the work:

```go
var sink int   // package-level: something OUTSIDE the function might read this later

func BenchmarkFibGood(b *testing.B) {
	var r int
	for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
		r = fib(30)
	}
	sink = r   // forces the compiler to treat r's value as genuinely used
}
```

The local variable `r` inside the loop, reassigned every iteration and only read once at the very end, is deliberately structured this way rather than assigning straight to `sink` inside the loop -- writing to a package-level variable on every single iteration would itself add extra work (and potentially its own synchronization concerns under `-race` or parallel benchmarks) that has nothing to do with what you're actually trying to measure. The rule to internalize: **any benchmark result that looks suspiciously, remarkably fast is a reason to check for a missing sink before celebrating a fast implementation.**

Sometimes a benchmark genuinely needs expensive setup -- building a large slice, populating a map, opening a file -- that you want to happen once, but whose cost you don't want counted as part of the per-operation measurement you actually care about:

```go
func BenchmarkSearchLargeSlice(b *testing.B) {
	data := buildLargeSortedSlice(1_000_000)   // expensive, and irrelevant to what we're measuring
	b.ResetTimer()                                // zero out elapsed time and allocation counters from setup, right now

	for i := 0; i < b.N; i++ {
		BinarySearch(data, 42)
	}
}
```

`b.ResetTimer()` zeroes out the benchmark's accumulated timing (and allocation counters) up to that point, without affecting anything that happens afterward -- so the expensive `buildLargeSortedSlice` call still runs (and still costs real wall-clock time during the actual benchmark execution), but that cost is simply excluded from the reported `ns/op`, which now reflects only the loop's own work. Remember from the first section that the whole benchmark function -- setup included -- may re-run several times during calibration; `b.ResetTimer()` needs to be called on every one of those re-runs to keep excluding setup consistently, which is automatically satisfied here since it's simply a line inside the function body, executed fresh every time the function itself runs.

For setup that needs to happen **repeatedly, inside the loop, once per iteration** rather than just once up front (say, each iteration needs a freshly-shuffled copy of data before the operation being measured), `b.StopTimer()` and `b.StartTimer()` bracket the excluded portion within the loop body itself: call `b.StopTimer()` before the per-iteration setup, do the setup, then `b.StartTimer()` immediately before the actual operation you want measured. This pair is used far less often than `ResetTimer`, because calling it inside a tight loop adds its own bookkeeping overhead per iteration -- reach for it only when in-loop setup genuinely cannot be hoisted out, and be aware that overusing it can itself distort the very measurement you're trying to isolate.

